    "confidence": 0.9,
    "explanation": "To both parties. This ruling concerns trip TRP-02816 (Tampines Mall to Blk 456 Hougang Ave 10, 30 Aug 2026), charged at a total of $30.55.\n\nThe ERP charge. The fare breakdown carries exactly one ERP line — AYE Alexandra Rd gantry, $3.00, charged 10:58:30 — and the optimal route's own ERP total is $0.00. Both the fare ledger and the platform's GPS analysis record that gantry as not on the optimal route. POL-RD-09 matches a gantry to the trip by treating any GPS fix within 250 m of it as a crossing. The trace is unimpaired for policy purposes — 162 fixes at a 10-second interval, 100% coverage, no gaps, maximum gap 0 seconds — so the match test can be run, and the closest the vehicle ever came to this gantry was 330.73 m (fix 128, 11:10:50). That is outside the 250 m radius, so the trace never passes the gantry and the line is a billing-integrity failure under POL-RD-09(4): the $3.00 is refunded in full as a standalone correction, available whether or not any deviation exists, and the matter is recorded for billing-integrity review. The $3.00 is below the SGD 8.00 automatic-execution threshold in POL-GEN-05 (and no safety or complaint flag applies), so the refund executes on issue.\n\nThe distance and time charges. The rider does not assert, and I do not find, a route deviation. The trace measures 20.58 km against a booked optimal of 19.6 km — an excess of 0.98 km, or 5.00%. POL-RD-01 requires the excess to exceed both 20% of optimal and 0.8 km, and the percentage test fails, so no deviation is established and POL-RD-02 does not run. I have accordingly not used the $26.72 recomputed fare or the $3.83 difference between it and the $30.55 charged total: that difference is driven by this disputed ERP line and by distance and time the deviation rules do not reach. The driver keeps the metered distance and time charges. There is also no unbilled-distance compensation for the driver: the ledger bills 20.6 km against a 20.58 km trace, so POL-RD-04's shortfall limb is not met.\n\nThe parties' statements and profiles. The driver's counter-statement — 'The ERP is charged by the system' — cites no evidence ID, names no gantry, road or route, and points to no artifact showing the vehicle crossed the gantry. Under POL-RD-08 it carries no weight and does not discharge the driver's burden on this charge. The bundle's traffic advisory (EV-ADVIS-02816-01) is MISSING with weight 0.0 and no payload, so no traffic explanation could have been tested against an advisory; because the driver named no advisory ID, that absence is a gap in the bundle and not an exculpatory clause invoked against a cited artifact, so POL-RD-10(5) is not engaged and the case does not escalate on this ground. On the profiles: the driver's deviation_flags_90d is 0 against 395 completed trips in 90 days, and the rider's deviation_claims_60d is 1 with 0 substantiated, so neither the POL-RD-06 pattern test nor POL-RD-08's extra-corroboration trigger is met. Neither profile supports an adverse finding against either party, and the lifetime counters and other context-only fields (prior_deviation_flags, prior_no_show_flags, prior_disputes_filed, prior_disputes_upheld) were not used as grounds, as POL-GEN-01 requires. No conduct referral arises under POL-RD-06(c).\n\nNo escalation. Trip identifiers match across the trip record, GPS trace and fare breakdown; the trace is not impaired under POL-RD-07(a)-(c); the timestamps are consistent; the refund is far below the SGD 150.00 ceiling in POL-RD-10(6); and the only charge line without a trace or trip-record counterpart is the ERP line refunded under POL-RD-09(4), which POL-RD-10(8) expressly excludes. No POL-RD-10 trigger fires.\n\nOutcome. PARTIAL_REFUND: $3.00 of the $5.00 contested is returned to the rider (the ERP pass-through correction), and the balance of the fare stands. The disputed amount is returned in part rather than in full, which is why the outcome is a partial refund rather than a full refund."
  },
